e the restarts are due to application errors. To check that, I should look into the logs of the pods that have restarts. The user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool, which can run kubectl logs. So, I should check the logs for the pods that have restarts to see if there are any errors or exceptions causing them to crash.\n\nLooking at the pod list, geo, profile, rate, recommendation, and reservation pods have had restarts. Let\'s pick one of them, maybe the geo pod, and check its logs. If there are errors in the logs, that could indicate the root cause. Alternatively, maybe the issue is with the deployments or replicasets. But since the pods are running, maybe the problem is with the application code or external dependencies.\n\nAnother approach could be to check the metrics using get_metrics to see if there are any anomalies, like high error rates or latency. But first, checking the logs would give direct insight into what\'s going wrong. So, the next step is to get the logs for the pods with restarts.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: Several pods (geo, profile, rate, recommendation, reservation) have non-zero restarts. To identify the root cause, I need to inspect their logs for errors or exceptions. Using `kubectl logs` on these pods will reveal application-level issues, such as crashes, failed dependencies, or misconfigurations. This is critical for diagnosing why pods are restarting despite being in "Running" state.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 406, 'prompt_tokens': 3567, 'total_tokens': 3973, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff23-eff4-7ab0-abdb-7776e1c8c560-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3567, 'output_tokens