 -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9798c933b52a80d2', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 286, 'prompt_tokens': 10794, 'total_tokens': 11080, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff24-bdb5-79c3-b9b2-259610153662-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe deployment reservation -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9798c933b52a80d2', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10794, 'output_tokens': 286, 'total_tokens': 11080}), ToolMessage(content='Name:                   reservation\nNamespace:              hotel-reservation\nCreationTimestamp:      Wed, 18 Mar 2026 04:07:45 +0000\nLabels:                 io.kompose.service=reservation\nAnnotations:            deployment.kubernetes.io/revision: 1\n                        kompose.cmd: kompose convert\n                        kompose.version: 1.22.0 (955b78124)\nSelector:               io.kompose.service=reservation\nReplicas:               1 desired | 1 updated | 1 total | 1 available | 0 unavailable\nStrategyType:           RollingUpdate\nMinReadySeconds:        0\nRollingUpdateStrategy:  25% max unavailable, 25% max surge\nPod Template:\n  Labels:       io.kompose.service=reservation\n  Annotations:  kompose.cmd: kompose convert\n                kompose.version: 1.22.0 (955b78124)\n                sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionPrefixes:\n                  cluster.outbound,cluster_manager,listener_manager,http_mixer_filter,tcp_mixer_filter,server,cluster.xds-grp,listener,connection_manager\n                sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionRegexps: http.*\n  Containers:\n   hotel-reserv-reservation:\n    Image:      yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest\n    Port:       8087/TCP\n    Host Port:  0/TCP\n    Command:\n      reservation\n    Limits:\n      cpu:  1\n    Requests:\n      cpu:  100m\n    Environment:\n      JAEGER_SAMPLE_RATIO:  1\n    Mounts:                 <none>\n  Volumes:                  <none>\n  Node-Selectors:           <none>\n  Tolerations:              <none>\nConditions:\n  Type           Status  Reason\n  ----           ------  ------\n  Progressing    True    NewReplicaSetAvailable\n  Available      True    MinimumReplicasAvailable\nOldReplicaSets:  <none>\nNewReplicaSet:   reservation-7bb77b596b (1/1 replicas created)\nEvents:\n  Type    Reason             Age    From                   Message\n  ----    ------             ----   ----                   -------\n  Normal  ScalingReplicaSet  4m23s  deployment-controller  Scaled up replica set reservation-7bb77b596b to 1\n', id='4db68569-389a-43aa-9567-a67408571950', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9798c933b52a80d2'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='2fa067df-147e-4046-a0bf-be20bc54397c'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why some microservices in the hotel-reservation application are restarting. So far, they checked the logs for geo, profile, rate, recommendation, and reservation pods. The logs for geo, profile, and rate didn\'t show any errors, but the recommendation and reservation deployments have restarts. \n\nLooking at the deployment descriptions for recommendation and reservation, both have 1 replica, and the events show they were scaled up. The pod status is "Running" but with restarts. The user needs to figure out why these pods are restarting despite being in a Running state.\n\nSince the logs didn\'t show errors, maybe the issue is with the application\'s health checks. The deployments don\'t mention liveness or readiness probes. If the probes are misconfigured or missing, the pods might be restarting due to failed health checks. Alternatively, there could be resource constraints, but the requests and limits set are 100m and 1 CPU, which might be sufficient. \n\nAnother angle: maybe the application itself is crashing due to some unhandled exceptions or external dependencies. The user should check the application\'s logs for any errors that might not be captured in the standard logs. Also, checking the metrics could help identify if there\'s a resource exhaustion issue like high CPU or memory usage. \n\nThe next step would be to check the metrics using get_metrics to see if there are any anomalies. For example, if the CPU usage is spiking, that could cause the pods to restart. Alternatively, if there are errors in the application\'s dependencies, like the database or memcached, that could lead to crashes. \n\nAlternatively, the user could check the events in the namespace for any recent issues. But since the user has already checked the pod logs and deployments, the next logical step is to look into metrics to see if there\'s a resource-related problem or if the application is encountering errors that aren\'t logged in the standard output.\n</think>\n\ntool name: get_metrics  \nreasoning: Since the **recommendation** and **reservation** pods have restarts but no clear errors in logs or deployments, I need to check real-time metrics (e.g., CPU, memory, error rates) to identify resource exhaustion, failed requests, or application-level crashes. This will help determine if the issue is due to resource constraints, misconfigured health checks, or external dependency failures.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 489, 'prompt_tokens': 10515, 'total_tokens': 11004, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff24-cf7f-7fd3-82be-9b34ce671017-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10515, 'output_tokens': 489, 'total_tokens': 11004}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6ea8ce90-e1e9-4fe5-b0c0-1531de732136'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose why the recommendation and reservation pods are restarting. They've checked the logs and deployments but didn't find obvious errors. Now they need to look into metrics.\n\nThe next step is to use get_metrics to check CPU and memory usage. If the pods are hitting resource limits, that could cause restarts. Also, checking for error rates or failed requests might show if there's an application issue. \n\nThe user should query Prometheus for metrics like CPU usage, memory usage, and possibly request latencies or error counts. The PromQL query should target the specific pods. For example, using sum by (pod) (container_cpu_usage_seconds_total) to see CPU usage. Similarly for memory. \n\nThey might also want to check if there are any failed HTTP requests or other anomalies. But since the pods are written in Go and using gRPC, maybe looking at specific application metrics like number of errors or request durations could help. \n\nThe user needs to construct the right PromQL queries. They should start with CPU and memory to rule out resource issues. If those are okay, then move to application-specific metrics. \n\nSo the tool call would be get_metrics with queries for CPU and memory usage of the affected pods.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "sum by (pod) (container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{pod!~\'(consul|jaeger|mongodb|memcached|search|user|frontend|geo|profile|rate|wrk2-job).*$\'})"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-to