clues about why they're restarting. \n\nAnother possibility is that the pods are restarting due to liveness or readiness probes failing. So checking the probe configurations would be important. But since the user has already checked the logs and didn't find errors, maybe the issue is with the deployment's resource requests or limits. \n\nThe user should next check the deployments for recommendation and reservation. Using kubectl describe deployment on those would show details about the replicas, resources, and any events that might indicate why the pods are restarting. If the deployments are configured with incorrect resource limits, that could cause the pods to crash and restart. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the services are dependent on other services that are not available, leading to crashes. But since the logs didn't show any connection issues, that might not be the case. \n\nSo the next tool call should be to describe the deployments for the recommendation and reservation services to check for any configuration issues or resource problems.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The **recommendation** and **reservation** pods also have restarts. Since logs for geo, profile, and rate show no errors, the issue might be with their deployments or resource constraints. Using `kubectl describe deployment` on these services will reveal details about replica statuses, resource limits, or configuration issues causing restarts. This is critical to identify if the problem is deployment-related (e.g., resource starvation, misconfigured probes) or external dependencies.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 524, 'prompt_tokens': 9074, 'total_tokens': 9598, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff24-753d-7773-886c-3397189771a9-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 9074, 'output_tokens': 524, 'total_tokens': 9598}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='f8ad85a7-b8e9-44bb-845c-55d8669c58a3'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose why some pods in the hotel-reservation namespace are restarting. They've already checked the logs for geo, profile, rate, and found no errors. Now they're looking at recommendation and reservation pods. The next step is to check their deployments.\n\nThe user mentioned using `kubectl describe deployment` to check replica statuses, resource limits, or configuration issues. But wait, the available tools include `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd`, which can run commands like `kubectl describe`. So, the correct approach is to run `kubectl describe deployment <deployment-name>` for the affected services.\n\nBut first, I need to know the deployment names. The services mentioned are recommendation and reservation. In Kubernetes, deployments are usually named similarly to the service. So, the deployments might be called recommendation and reservation as well. \n\nThe user should run `kubectl describe deployment recommendation -n hotel-reservation` and the same for reservation. But since the user can only make one tool call at a time, they should start with one. Let's pick recommendation first. \n\nSo the tool call would be to execute `kubectl describe deployment recommendation -n hotel-reservation`. This will show details about the deployment, including replicas, events, and any errors related to the deployment. If there are issues like resource limits being exceeded, or failed probes, this command would highlight them. \n\nAlternatively, if the deployment isn't set up correctly, the describe command would show the current state, like if the replicas are not available, or if there are rollout errors. This is a crucial step to check if the problem is with the deployment configuration rather than the pods themselves.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe deployment recommendation -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b769cd3af3844e4b', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 373, 'prompt_tokens': 10083, 'total_tokens': 10456, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff24-8f84-7ee0-8402-673a1b54477e-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe deployment recommendation -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b769cd3af3844e4b', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10083, 'output_tokens': 373, 'total_tokens': 10456}), ToolMessage(content='Name:                   recommendation\nNamespace:              hotel-reservation\nCreationTimestamp:      Wed, 18 Mar 2026 04:07:36 +0000\nLabels:                 io.kompose.service=recommendation\nAnnotations:            deployment.kubernetes.io/revision: 1\n                        kompose.cmd: kompose convert\n                        kompose.version: 1.22.0 (955b78124)\nSelector:               io.kompose.service=recommendation\nReplicas:               1 desired | 1 updated | 1 total | 1 available | 0 unavailable\nStrategyType:           RollingUpdate\nMinReadySeconds:        0\nRollingUpdateStrategy:  25% max unavailable, 25% max surge\nPod Template:\n  Labels:       io.kompose.service=recommendation\n  Annotations:  kompose.cmd: kompose convert\n                kompose.version: 1.22.0 (955b78124)\n                sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionPrefixes:\n                  cluster.outbound,cluster_manager,listener_manager,http_mixer_filter,tcp_mixer_filter,server,cluster.xds-grp,listener,connection_manager\n                sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionRegexps: http.*\n  Containers:\n   hotel-reserv-recommendation:\n    Image:      yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest\n    Port:       8085/TCP\n    Host Port:  0/TCP\n    Command:\n      recommendation\n    Limits:\n      cpu:  1\n    Requests:\n      cpu:  100m\n    Environment:\n      JAEGER_SAMPLE_RATIO:  1\n    Mounts:                 <none>\n  Volumes:                  <none>\n  Node-Selectors:           <none>\n  Tolerations:              <none>\nConditions:\n  Type           Status  Reason\n  ----           ------  ------\n  Progressing    True    NewReplicaSetAvailable\n  Available      True    MinimumReplicasAvailable\nOldReplicaSets:  <none>\nNewReplicaSet:   recommendation-64754c9c4f (1/1 replicas created)\nEvents:\n  Type    Reason             Age    From                   Message\n  ----    ------             ----   ----                   -------\n  Normal  ScalingReplicaSet  4m20s  deployment-controller  Scaled up replica set recommendation-64754c9c4f to 1\n', id='0e73fe51-e308-475d-b306-8f2773e0e1ef', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b769cd3af3844e4b'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='3a8de386-94c1-4a5a-bda4-bed4b9696d37'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose why some pods in the hotel-reservation namespace are restarting. They've checked the logs for geo, profile, rate, recommendation, and reservation pods. The logs for geo, profile, and rate don't show any errors, so the issue might be with the recommendation and reservation deployments.\n\nThe user ran `kubectl describe deployment recommendation` and saw that the deployment has 1 replica, and the pod is running. But the pod has restarted twice. The next step is to check the reservation deployment as well. Maybe the reservation deployment has similar issues. \n\nLooking at the previous steps, the user checked the recommendation deployment. Now they should check the reservation deployment to see if there are any issues with its configuration, resource limits, or if there are events indicating problems. \n\nAlternatively, they could check the pod's status and events for the reservation service. Using `kubectl describe pod` on the reservation pod might show more details about why it's restarting. The user should check the pod's events to see if there are any errors or resource constraints. \n\nAnother possibility is that the pods are restarting due to liveness or readiness probes failing. The user should check the deployment's probe configurations. If the probes are misconfigured, the pods might be restarted even if the application is running. \n\nBut since the user has already checked the logs and the deployment description for recommendation, the next logical step is to check the reservation deployment's describe output. If the reservation deployment also has similar settings, that could indicate a pattern. \n\nAlternatively, checking the resource requests and limits in the deployment's YAML might show if the pods are being terminated due to resource constraints. The user should also check if there are any events related to the reservation pod that could indicate resource exhaustion or other issues. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to describe the reservation deployment to gather more information about its configuration and any potential issues.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The **reservation** pod also has restarts. To investigate its deployment configuration, I'll use `kubectl describe deployment reservation` to check for resource limits, probe failures, or deployment anomalies. This will help determine if the issue is deployment-specific (e.g., resource constraints, misconfigured liveness/readiness probes) or external.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 474, 'prompt_tokens': 9810, 'total_tokens': 10284, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff24-a62d-7880-92ee-1c62131b01c1-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 9810, 'output_tokens': 474, 'total_tokens': 10284}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='81a94fa4-1ce8-4e43-8805-f1f4d6675079'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the hotel reservation application, which consists of multiple microservices. So far, they've checked the pods and found that several services like geo, profile, rate, recommendation, and reservation have restarts. They checked the logs for geo, profile, and rate, but didn't find any errors. Then they looked into the deployment for recommendation and found that it's running with one replica, no issues in the events.\n\nNow, the next step is to check the reservation deployment. The user is using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool to describe the deployment for reservation. The previous step showed that the recommendation deployment had no issues, but reservation might have different settings. By checking the reservation deployment, they can see if there are any resource limits, probe configurations, or other deployment settings that might be causing the restarts. If the reservation deployment has similar settings to recommendation, maybe there's a different issue. Alternatively, if there are differences in resource requests or limits, that could explain the restarts. Also, checking the events related to the reservation deployment might show scaling issues or other problems. So, the next tool call should be to describe the reservation deployment to gather more details.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe deployment reservation -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9798c933b52a80d2', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 286, 'prompt_tokens': 10794, 'total_tokens': 11080, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff24-bdb5-79c3-b9b2-259610153662-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe deployment reservation -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9798c933b52a80d2', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10794, 'output_tokens': 286, 'total_tokens': 11080}), ToolMessage(content='Name:                   reservation\nNamespace:              hotel-reservation\nCreationTimestamp:      Wed, 18 Mar 2026 04:07:45 +0000\nLabels:                 io.kompose.service=reservation\nAnnotations:            deployment.kubernetes.io/revision: 1\n                        kompose.cmd: kompose convert\n                        kompose.version: 1.22.0 (955b78124)\nSelector:               io.kompose.service=reservation\nReplicas:               1 desired | 1 updated | 1 total | 1 available | 0 unavailable\nStrategyType:           RollingUpdate\nMinReadySeconds:        0\nRollingUpdateStrategy:  25% max unavailable, 25% max surge\nPod Template:\n  Labels:       io.kompose.service=reservation\n  Annotations:  kompose.cmd: kompose convert\n                kompose.version: 1.22.0 (955b78124)\n                sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionPrefixes:\n                  cluster.outbound,cluster_manager,listener_manager,http_mixer_filter,tcp_mixer_filter,server,cluster.xds-grp,listener,connection_manager\n                sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionRegexps: http.*\n  Containers:\n   hotel-reserv-reservation:\n    Image:      yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest\n    Port:       8087/TCP\n    Host Port:  0/TCP\n    Command:\n      reservation\n    Limits:\n      cpu:  1\n    Requests:\n      cpu:  100m\n    Environment:\n      JAEGER_SAMPLE_RATIO:  1\n    Mounts:                 <none>\n  Volumes:                  <none>\n  Node-Selectors:           <none>\n  Tolerations:              <none>\nConditions:\n  Type           Status  Reason\n  ----           ------  ------\n  Progressing    True    NewReplicaSetAvailable\n  Available      True    MinimumReplicasAvailable\nOldReplicaSets:  <none>\nNewReplicaSet:   reservation-7bb77b596b (1/1 replicas created)\nEvents:\n  Type    Reason             Age    From                   Message\n  ----    ------             ----   ----                   -------\n  Normal  ScalingReplicaSet  4m23s  deployment-controller  Scaled up replica set reservation-7bb77b596b to 1\n', id='4db68569-389a-43aa-9567-a67408571950', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9798c933b52a80d2'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='2fa067df-147e-4046-a0bf-be20bc54397c'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why some microservices in the hotel-reservation application are restarting. So far, they checked the logs for geo, profile, rate, recommendation, and reservation pods. The logs for geo, profile, and rate didn\'t show any errors, but the recommendation and reservation deployments have restarts. \n\nLooking at the deployment descriptions for recommendation and reservation, both have 1 replica, and the events show they were scaled up. The pod status is "Running" but with restarts. The user needs to figure out why these pods are restarting despite being in a Running state.\n\nSince the logs didn\'t show errors, maybe the issue is with the application\'s health checks. The deployments don\'t mention liveness or readiness probes. If the probes are misconfigured or missing, the pods might be restarting due to failed health checks. Alternatively, there could be resource constraints, but the requests and limits set are 100m and 1 CPU, which might be sufficient. \n\nAnother angle: maybe the application itself is crashing due to some unhandled exceptions or external dependencies. The user should check the application\'s logs for any errors that might not be captured in the standard logs. Also, checking the metrics could help identify if there\'s a resource exhaustion issue like high CPU or memory usage. \n\nThe next step would be to check the metrics using get_metrics to see if there are any anomalies. For example, if the CPU usage is spiking, that could cause the pods to restart. Alternatively, if there are errors in the application\'s dependencies, like the database or memcached, that could lead to crashes. \n\nAlternatively, the user could check the events in the namespace for any recent issues. But since the user has already checked the pod logs and deployments, the next logical step is to look into metrics to see if there\'s a resource-related problem or if the application is encountering errors that aren\'t logged in the standard output.\n</think>\n\ntool name: get_metrics  \nreasoning: Since the **recommendation** and **reservation** pods have restarts but no clear errors in logs or deployments, I need to check real-time metrics (e.g., CPU, memory, error rates) to identify resource exhaustion, failed requests, or application-level crashes. This will help determine if the issue is due to resource constraints, misconfigured health checks, or external dependency failures.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 489, 'prompt_tokens': 10515, 'total_tokens': 11004, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff24-cf7f-7fd3-82be-9b34ce671017-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10515, 'output_tokens': 489, 'total_tokens': 11004}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6ea8ce90-e1e9-4fe5-b0c0-1531de732136'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose why the recommendation and reservation pods are restarting. They've checked the logs and deployments but didn't find obvious errors. Now they need to look into metrics.\n\nThe next step is to use get_metrics to check CPU and memory usage. If the pods are hitting resource limits, that could cause restarts. Also, checking for error rates or failed requests might show if there's an application issue. \n\nThe user should query Prometheus for metrics like CPU usage, memory usage, and possibly request latencies or error counts. The PromQL query should target the specific pods. For example, using sum by (pod) (container_cpu_usage_seconds_total) to see CPU usage. Similarly for memory. \n\nThey might also want to check if there are any failed HTTP requests or other anomalies. But since the pods are written in Go and using gRPC, maybe looking at specific application metrics like number of errors or request durations could help. \n\nThe user needs to construct the right PromQL queries. They should start with CPU and memory to rule out resource issues. If those are okay, then move to application-specific metrics. \n\nSo the tool call would be get_metrics with queries for CPU and memory usage of the affected pods.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "sum by (pod) (container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{pod!~\'(consul|jaeger|mongodb|memcached|search|user|frontend|geo|profile|rate|wrk2-job).*$\'})"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-abc2c2b4f85b5753', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 323, 'prompt_tokens': 11506, 'total_tokens': 11829, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff24-e7e1-7c10-a9ea-de515d6c8f6a-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': "sum by (pod) (container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{pod!~'(consul|jaeger|mongodb|memcached|search|user|frontend|geo|profile|rate|wrk2-job).*$'})"}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-abc2c2b4f85b5753', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11506, 'output_tokens': 323, 'total_tokens': 11829}), ToolMessage(content='("{\'resultType\': \'vector\', \'result\': [{\'metric\': {}, \'value\': [1773807139.157, \'1085271.0577170013\']}, {\'metric\': {\'pod\': \'kube-proxy-8qqrf\'}, \'value\': [1773807139.157, \'300.477328\']}, {\'metric\': {\'pod\': \'promtail-fbnpd\'}, \'value\': [1773807139.157, \'243.781894\']}, {\'metric\': {\'pod\': \'openebs-ndm-operator-cb7bf6676-cg8dh\'}, \'value\': [1773807139.157, \'6.366246\']}, {\'metric\': {\'pod\': \'init-pvc-ed537f71-c058-4fdb-8ed6-49f273418b81\'}, \'value\': [1773807139.157, \'0.053407\']}, {\'metric\': {\'pod\': \'prometheus-alertmanager-0\'}, \'value\': [1773807139.157, \'9.564481\']}, {\'metric\': {\'pod\': \'prometheus-prometheus-node-exporter-kzp5r\'}, \'value\': [1773807139.157, \'18.866232\']}, {\'metric\': {\'pod\': \'loki-canary-m6m7s\'}, \'value\': [1773807139.157, \'14.12435\