ibe deployment to check for probe settings. But the user already did that for the recommendation deployment. \n\nLooking back, the user described the recommendation deployment and didn\'t see any probe settings mentioned. So maybe the probes are not configured, and the BackOff is due to something else. \n\nAlternatively, the user could check the pod\'s status again for any additional events. \n\nBut given the available tools, the next step is to check the probe configurations. Since the user can\'t get the deployment YAML, maybe they can use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the deployment\'s YAML. \n\nWait, the available tools include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. So the user can run "kubectl get deployment recommendation -n hotel-reservation -o yaml" to get the full deployment configuration, including probe settings. \n\nYes, that\'s a good next step. The user hasn\'t checked the full deployment YAML yet, so they can look for liveness or readiness probes. If those are misconfigured, that could be the root cause. \n\nSo the next tool call should be to get the deployment\'s YAML to check for probe configurations.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The **recommendation** pod has a non-zero exit code (2) and a "BackOff" event, indicating a liveness/readiness probe failure. To confirm, I\'ll retrieve the full deployment YAML for **recommendation** to check if liveness/readiness probes are misconfigured, which could cause the pod to restart despite appearing "Running." This will help identify if probe failures are the root cause.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1542, 'prompt_tokens': 16474, 'total_tokens': 18016, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff25-73ea-7bc0-b3a4-c